WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.919
What do the founders and leaders of Tesla, 
YouTube, and LinkedIn have in common?

00:00:04.919 --> 00:00:09.100
They all started their early careers at PayPal,
the online payment company.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:13.070
This group of highly ambitious twenty and
thirty-year-olds has been dubbed the “PayPal

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:17.640
mafia” and once even dressed up as mafia
members for the cover of Fortune magazine.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.800
They went on to found or run some of the largest
tech companies in the world.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.829
Elon Musk was absent the day of the shoot
because of a scheduling conflict.

00:00:25.829 --> 00:00:27.310
This is by no means all of them.

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:31.749
Peter Thiel estimates
that the “mafia” is around 220 people.

00:00:31.749 --> 00:00:35.520
Thiel is a famous start-up investor
often referred to as the “don” of the

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:36.840
PayPal mafia.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:41.940
PayPal began when Thiel and Max Levchin created
software company Confinity as a way to transfer

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.260
money through the once-popular Palm Pilot.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:49.640
Confinity then merged with Elon Musk’s payment
company x.com, to become PayPal in 2000.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:54.530
Musk briefly served as CEO before being kicked
out in favor of Thiel who took over until

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:59.160
eBay bought PayPal in 2002 for $1.5 billion dollars.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:03.440
It was partly eBay’s doing for making 
these entrepreneurs so successful.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.399
When eBay bought PayPal, many of the PayPal
employees left because they had difficulty

00:01:07.399 --> 00:01:10.869
adjusting to the traditional corporate culture
at eBay.

00:01:10.869 --> 00:01:15.369
Within four years, only 12 of the first 50
PayPal employees remained.

00:01:15.369 --> 00:01:19.340
The rest took their entrepreneurial skills
to make the next great thing.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.130
And because they were friends they could lean
on each other to build new companies.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:26.220
Thiel and Levchin made a habit of hiring people
they liked.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:29.149
Thiel recruited a bunch of Stanford 
grads who attended around

00:01:29.149 --> 00:01:30.729
the same time as him.

00:01:30.729 --> 00:01:35.179
Levchin scouted developers and former classmates
from his alma mater, the University of Illinois

00:01:35.179 --> 00:01:37.159
Urbana-Champaign.

00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:40.170
Levchin once asked a guy in an interview what
he liked to do for fun.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:41.929
He responded: play hoops.

00:01:41.929 --> 00:01:45.469
Levchin decided not to hire him because he
said everyone he knew in college who played

00:01:45.469 --> 00:01:47.450
basketball was an idiot.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:51.439
They weeded out the jocks, frat members, and
those with MBAs.

00:01:51.439 --> 00:01:53.880
Instead, they hired people like themselves.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:58.020
Levchin described the ideal candidate:
“He thinks like me, he’s just as geeky,

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.579
and he doesn’t get laid very often.

00:02:00.579 --> 00:02:01.579
Great hire!

00:02:01.579 --> 00:02:02.579
We’ll get along perfectly.”

00:02:02.579 --> 00:02:05.649
And very importantly, they hired workaholics.

00:02:05.649 --> 00:02:10.479
A start-up involves long hours and a lot
of pressure, but not a lot of sleep.

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:15.010
The pace at PayPal was so intense that employees
didn’t have a life outside of work.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:17.260
They were each others' social life.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:21.700
Most of the friends who showed up to Levchin’s
29th birthday at an upscale Vietnamese restaurant

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.310
in San Francisco were former PayPal colleagues.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:28.629
At one point the conversation turned to how
hard it was to find a good dentist.

00:02:28.629 --> 00:02:33.129
That got Jeremy Stoppelman, a former VP of
engineering at PayPal, and Russel Simmons,

00:02:33.129 --> 00:02:36.930
one of the brilliant engineers behind PayPal,
talking about a website where people could

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:37.930
review services.

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:40.040
They would go on to found Yelp.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:44.090
The problems they solved at PayPal served
them well as they built their own companies.

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:48.489
They had to figure out how to stop PayPal
from losing millions of dollars each month.

00:02:48.489 --> 00:02:52.700
They had to deal with fraud, stiff competition
from credit card companies as well as eBay’s

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:54.460
own payment system.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:58.940
As Reid Hoffman, a former executive vice-president
at PayPal put it: “Nothing focuses your

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.416
attention quite like losing money.”

00:03:01.416 --> 00:03:03.810
And focus was the name of the game.

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:07.989
Former PayPal executive Keith Rabois explained
on the question and answer site Quora that

00:03:07.989 --> 00:03:13.780
PayPal was driven by the “extreme focus”
of each individual on a single task.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:19.230
There was great emphasis on individual accomplishment
and as a result, COO David Sacks wasn’t

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.250
a fan of big meetings.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:25.150
This is something Elon Musk has taken 
to heart as CEO of Tesla.

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:30.230
He reportedly sent an email to Tesla employees
saying large meetings are a waste of time.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:34.380
And people are free to walk out of one or
drop off a call if they’re not adding anything

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:35.430
of value.

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:40.709
Musk took his $180 million in PayPal earnings
from the eBay sale to become one of the major

00:03:40.709 --> 00:03:45.019
funders of Tesla while also 
starting his rocket company SpaceX.

00:03:45.019 --> 00:03:48.079
Others put their money into supporting 
startups by PayPal alumni.

00:03:48.079 --> 00:03:49.940
That’s how YouTube began.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:54.130
Three former PayPal employees came up with
the idea for a video sharing platform when

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:57.959
they couldn’t find any videos of the famous
Superbowl halftime incident involving

00:03:57.959 --> 00:04:01.400
Justin Timberlake and Janet Jackson in 2004.

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:04.470
Chad Hurley designed the logo 
and look of YouTube.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:06.060
Steve Chen made sure it worked.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:10.170
And Jawed Karim helped with both the design
and the programming.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:15.569
Karim uploaded the first-ever video to YouTube,
“Me at the zoo”, on April 23, 2005 which

00:04:15.569 --> 00:04:19.430
now has almost 200 million views!

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:23.930
But the bond between three PayPal bros wasn’t
enough to make YouTube successful.

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:25.840
They needed financial backing.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:28.970
And that’s where their PayPal connections
came in handy.

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:34.020
While at a backyard BBQ, Karim showed the
site to his friend, Rabois, the former PayPal

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:35.020
executive.

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:38.310
Rabois later told PayPal’s former chief
financial officer, Roelof Bathoa, about it.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:42.800
Bathoa is a partner at Sequoia Capital,
a venture capital firm that has backed big

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.060
names like Apple and Google.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:47.780
Botha then got Sequoia to invest in YouTube.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:52.490
Some PayPal executives went on to become prolific
angel investors, like Hoffman.

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:53.490
If you wanted to start a company, this was
the guy to know.

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:57.590
He reportedly arranged the first meeting between
Thiel and Mark Zuckerberg which led Thiel

00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:03.310
to smartly invest half a million dollars into
Facebook in return for a 10% stake.

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:06.130
Thiel would walk away with more than $1 billion
dollars.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:09.330
There were few investors looking to write
cheques at the time.

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:14.430
The dot-com bubble of the late nineties destroyed
the dreams of many when it burst in the early

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:15.430
2000s.

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:20.139
There was even talk in Silicon Valley that
consumer tech might be dead.

00:05:20.139 --> 00:05:22.950
PayPal was one of the few start-ups to survive
the crash.

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:25.055
And not only survive but thrive

00:05:25.055 --> 00:05:28.615
which goes to show how important
networking and surrounding yourself with the

00:05:28.615 --> 00:05:30.800
right people really are.

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:36.220
The PayPal mafia went on to create seven “unicorn”
companies - privately held start-ups valued

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:37.690
at over $1 billion.

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:43.139
There’s another unicorn I haven’t mentioned
yet which brings me to the sponsor of my video,

00:05:43.139 --> 00:05:44.139
